  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 530 PM EDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nRidging remains the dominant synoptic-scale weather feature across \nthe Eastern Kentucky for the duration of the long term forecast \nperiod. When the period opens on Wednesday morning, a nearly 600 \ndecameter ridge will be centered over the Tennessee River Valley, or \njust to the south of the forecast area. The strength of this ridge \nis quite impressive. These modeled geopotential heights rank among \nthe upper echelon of model climatology for this time of year. In \nfact, mean LREF heights of 600 dm sit at the 100th percentile of \nthe dataset. This signal has been present in the data for multiple\nruns in a row, which bolsters forecast confidence. The proximity \nof this anomalously strong mid/upper atmospheric ridging should \naccordingly yield mostly dry and unseasonably warm sensible \nweather conditions across Eastern Kentucky. On Wednesday, expect \nhigh temperatures in the lower 90s, mostly sunny skies, efficient \ndiurnal mixing processes, and overnight ridge-valley temperature \nsplits. Valley lows in the mid/upper 60s should be sufficient for \npatchy fog formation near sources of water, while ridgetops and \nthe more exposed western locales could stay closer to the 70 \ndegree mark.\n\nConfidence in these dry/hot conditions is highest on Wednesday, as \nthe parent ridging will be closest to the JKL CWA in this time \nframe. Large-scale subsidence and antecedent dry air throughout the \ncolumn will largely limit the potential for meaningful convection in \nthe initial stages of the period. The dry air (and mixed afternoon \ndewpoints) should also prevent Eastern Kentucky from reaching heat \nadvisory criteria. Apparent temperatures peak in the upper 90s, but \nremain largely below the triple digit mark. The heat continues into \nThursday, with similar forecast highs in the lower 90s and heat \nindices in the upper 90s. While this may be enough to break the \nrecord high at the Jackson climate site, this is only because the \nprevious record is a \"weak\" 90 degrees. The pattern actually looks \nto shift a little bit on Thursday, and that shift will eventually \nculminate in relief from the heat.   \n\nAs the ridge retrogrades west a little bit, return flow around its \nnorthern periphery will advect a plume of modified Gulf moisture \ntowards the CWA on Thursday. The presence of this moisture will \nlikely make it feel a couple of degrees warmer outside in the \nwestern third of the CWA. Those western counties will remain closer \nto the center of the ridge and thus under more of its influence. \nLocales situated in the Bluegrass and near Lake Cumberland could \nflirt with triple digit heat indices on Thursday, but this is \nunlikely in counties further to the east. Isolated to scattered \ngeneral thunderstorm chances re-enter the forecast on Thursday \nafternoon for locations along and east of the Pottsville Escarpment. \nSimilar, sub-50% PoPs remain in the grids through the weekend, \nlargely modulated by the diurnal instability cycle. These could \nprovide some localized pockets of relief from the heat before a more \nrobust pattern shift emerges early next week. The forecast guidance \nsuite continues to disagree on how exactly that pattern change \nunfolds, but there is a notable increase in the spread between the \n25th and 75th NBM temperature quartiles on Sunday and Monday. This \naffirms the notion that the increased rain chances correspond with \nincreased forecast uncertainty surrounding the heat in the latter \nstages of the period. However, there is a better-defined downward \ntrend in the temperatures early next week, with more compact model \nspread and median values back in the upper 70s/near 80 degrees.",
